            {
              "reviewerRole": "product-manager",
              "actor": "product-manager",
              "verdict": "sendBack",
              "summary": "Single blocking defect: all five objectives in the draft are empty. Each has number 0 and statement \"\". The commit message for the draft mentions 5 business objectives, so they were either lost or never written into the model. Objectives are the WHY, and the mission can't be judged against them while they're blank.\n\nRequired change: write five objectives with numbers 1-5 and non-empty statements. Each should say why the business wants the vision, in business or user language, for example what the team gains from trusting the list, what the business avoids, and what it gets from keeping the scope small. They must not restate the vision or the mission. Don't use component, module, service, subsystem, layer or any other decomposition terms, and don't imply a breakdown into parts.\n\nChecked and fine, so don't change them:\n- The vision is one terse sentence (about 15 words) with no technology named and no benefit-tail.\n- The mission states an operating approach: one small, complete experience that behaves the same everywhere, with each person's list kept beyond a session, and a scope limited to the essentials. It is in business language and doesn't restate the vision.\n- Neither the vision nor the mission uses architecture vocabulary.\n\nTaste-level only, not a reason to revise: \"every way of reaching it\" is slightly abstract. Keep it unless the objectives make a clearer phrasing natural.",
